# kanshi — declarative output profiles, reapplied on hotplug.
#
# This lives in the SHARED `config' package, not in `sway' or `river', because
# monitor geometry is a property of the hardware rather than of a compositor.
#
# WHO ACTUALLY RUNS IT
#
#   river  — ALWAYS. river has no output configuration of its own; river(1)
#            explicitly says "kanshi(1) could be started to manage output
#            configuration". ~/.config/river/init starts it.
#
#   sway   — NO, by default. sway configures outputs itself in
#            sway/host-basedserv.conf, and kanshi would fight it: whichever ran
#            last wins, and they can ping-pong. Exactly one thing must own
#            output state. To hand ownership to kanshi under sway instead,
#            comment out the two `output' blocks in host-basedserv.conf and
#            `systemctl --user enable --now kanshi.service'.
#
# GEOMETRY
#
# Reproduces x11/.screenlayout/g9andcrt.sh:
#
#   xrandr --output DP-0   --mode 5120x1440 -r 240 --dpi 100 --pos 0x0
#          --output HDMI-0 --mode 800x600            --pos 4200x1440
#
# Note X11 and Wayland name these differently. X calls them DP-0 / HDMI-0; the
# kernel connector names, which wlroots uses, are DP-1 / HDMI-A-1. Confirm with
# `wlr-randr` inside a river session.
#
#   x=0                                              x=5120
#    +----------------------------------------------+  y=0
#    |          DP-1 — Samsung Odyssey G95SC         |
#    |               5120 x 1440 @ 240               |
#    +----------------------------------------------+  y=1440
#                               +--------+
#                               | HDMI   |  800x600 at x=4200
#                               +--------+  y=2040
#
# The small panel sits BELOW the G9 and horizontally inset, not beside it.
# Non-overlapping, so pointer transitions behave.
#
# REFRESH RATE — the one thing to check first if this looks wrong
#
# The refresh rate MUST be stated explicitly. From the G9's EDID, its
# 5120x1440 modes are:
#
#   239.999110 Hz
#   119.978835 Hz   <-- PREFERRED
#    59.999500 Hz
#
# The panel's *preferred* mode is 120 Hz, so a bare `mode 5120x1440' would
# silently give you half the refresh rate you asked for.
#
# `@240Hz' below is the rounded, human value. I could not verify on this
# hardware whether kanshi matches refresh rates exactly or to the nearest
# supported one — it reports them to six decimal places internally
# ("doesn't support mode '1920x1080@0.000000Hz'"), which suggests it may be
# strict. If the G9 comes up at 120 Hz, that is the cause; use the exact value:
#
#   output DP-1 mode 5120x1440@239.999110Hz position 0,0 scale 1
#
# Verify in a live session with:
#
#   sudo pacman -S --needed wlr-randr
#   wlr-randr                 # lists every mode with its true refresh
#   journalctl --user -t river -b | grep -i kanshi
#
# kanshi logs "doesn't support mode ..." when it rejects one, so this fails
# loudly rather than silently.
#
# Profiles are matched in order and the first whose outputs all match wins, so
# the most specific must come first.

# Both displays — the normal desk setup.
profile g9andcrt {
    output DP-1 mode 5120x1440@240Hz position 0,0 scale 1
    output HDMI-A-1 mode 800x600 position 4200,1440 scale 1
}

# G9 alone — mirrors .screenlayout/g9.sh.
profile g9 {
    output DP-1 mode 5120x1440@240Hz position 0,0 scale 1
}

# Small panel alone, e.g. if the G9 is off or on another input.
profile crtonly {
    output HDMI-A-1 mode 800x600 position 0,0 scale 1
}
